From 35efcd3bc5db590a99c7a967503b4b296326d303 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Volker Badziong <development@richtfest.tech>
Date: Tue, 21 Jul 2026 03:59:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 06/10] Stage per-server scope properties across server startup

A game server's load-time peak routinely dwarfs its steady working set --
world generation and asset streaming reach for everything at once -- so a
memory.high sized for the steady state throttles exactly the phase that can
least afford it. Worse, the damage is permanent: memory.min and memory.low
only fend off reclaim driven from OUTSIDE the cgroup, so reclaim a cgroup
inflicts on itself by exceeding its own memory.high ignores them entirely. A
ceiling below the load-time peak evicts the server straight through its own
floor, and those pages come back only as the workload faults them in again.

This stages a separate startup memory band (startup_defaults /
WINGS_CG_STARTUP_*) that holds until the server reports ready -- the egg's
own startup matcher, or WINGS_CG_STEADY_MATCH where "done" fires before the
world has finished loading -- or until startup_grace expires, whichever comes
first. The steady ceiling is then reached by a self-pacing ramp
(steady_ramp_step) that walks memory.high down and pauses at each step until
reclaim catches up, instead of slamming it in one shot. Only the memory band
is staged: cpu/io weights are work-conserving and cannot evict anything.
WINGS_CG_PHASE_EVENTS optionally mirrors named console lines into the Panel
activity log.

Adapted for scope-targeted properties. The startup band used to be applied in
OnBeforeStart, just before the container started, because its slice already
existed by then. A container scope does not exist until the container's init
process is launched, so the band is applied immediately after
ContainerStart() instead -- the same property sets, one step later.

The property applier now takes the band to apply. Its two call sites in
environment/docker are power transitions of the container itself -- the apply
that follows ContainerStart, and the re-assert made when Wings re-attaches to
a container that outlived it -- so the band is unambiguous at both.

The panel-side re-assertion added with the scope work therefore moves UP, out
of Environment.InSituUpdate and into its caller, Server.SyncWithEnvironment.
An Environment can only see Docker's process state, and that is not the slice
phase: a server with an explicit WINGS_CG_STEADY_MATCH deliberately stays in
its startup band long after the environment reports running -- for a
world-streaming game that is the whole remaining world load, bounded only by
startup_grace -- and the grace backstop conversely moves a server to steady
while the environment is still starting. Inferring the band from process
state would apply the steady one, unramped, to a server at its load-time
peak, which is the eviction this patch exists to prevent, and it would stand
for the rest of that boot because nothing re-applies the startup band.

A re-assertion differs from a phase transition in two further ways, both in
Server.reassertSliceProps. It has no "nothing staged" early exit: skipping a
transition between two identical bands is right, skipping the repair of those
same properties is not, because the premise is that what is on the unit is no
longer what Wings wrote. And it leaves memory.high alone. That is the one
property a foreign writer cannot have damaged -- Docker's container.Resources
has no MemoryHigh field, so runc never writes it -- and the one a repair could
do damage with, by writing a resting ceiling onto a workload still above it,
mid-load or mid-ramp. Everything Docker's write can reach is re-asserted.

The phase no longer pins a unit. Pinning existed because placement was fixed
at container-create time, so a Panel sync changing WINGS_CGROUP_PARENT
mid-startup could leave the steady band on a slice the container was not in.
The scope name is now read back from the container that actually exists at
apply time, so it cannot drift; what remains is a plain in-progress flag.

Two things a reader should be able to check rather than take on trust, so
both are tests. dispatchSliceProps reaches the outside world through two
package variables, which lets a unit test drive SyncWithEnvironment against a
stub environment and assert the request that actually came out: without that
seam, deleting the re-assertion outright, or rewiring it through the
transition path (which restores the "nothing staged" exit), both leave the
whole suite green. And the claim that Docker cannot reach memory.high is a
claim about a struct in another module that nothing in the compiler defends,
so a reflection test walks container.Resources and container.UpdateConfig and
fails if a docker bump ever adds a field that could.

